    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm working on a 12 week hypertrophy block for a client but they've hit a serious plateau on their back squat. they haven't added any weight in 3 weeks and their RPE is hitting 10 way too early. can you explain how deload weeks and changing up the volume might help them break through this? thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A deload week allows your client's central nervous system and muscles to recover from intense training stress, which can directly help break through a plateau. By reducing volume and intensity temporarily, their body can adapt and come back stronger when you reintroduce heavier loads. Changing up volume, perhaps by cycling high volume/low intensity with low volume/high intensity, can also introduce novel stimuli your nervous system hasn't adapted to. This varied approach prevents accommodation and re-challenges the muscles in a fresh way.",
      "source": "target",
